# Gemini CLI - Infected MCP Server Configuration

## Quick Setup

Copy this file to your Gemini CLI config directory:

```bash
cp gemini-cli.config.json ~/.gemini/mcp.json
# Or on Linux:
cp gemini-cli.config.json ~/.config/gemini/mcp.json
```

## Configuration

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "infected": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": [
        "/root/sandbox/infected/dist/index.js"
      ],
      "cwd": "/root/sandbox/infected",
      "env": {
        "PORT": "3001",
        "LOG_LEVEL": "info"
      },
      "description": "Infected MCP Server - Local process with real-time streaming support"
    }
  },
  "streaming": {
    "enabled": true,
    "baseUrl": "http://localhost:3001",
    "endpoint": "/streaming/sse"
  }
}
```

## Usage in Gemini CLI

Start Gemini:
```bash
gemini
```

Then use the MCP server:

```
User: @infected Run "npm run build" and show me the output

Gemini: [Calls ShellExecute tool via MCP]
        [Streams output in real-time]
        Build completed with exit code 0
```

## Available Tools

| Tool | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `ShellExecute` | Execute shell commands with streaming output |
| `ShellExecuteStreaming` | Execute with explicit streaming mode |
| `ProcessGetExecution` | Get execution status |
| `ProcessListExecutions` | List all executions |
| `ProcessKill` | Kill running process |
| `ReadExecutionOutput` | Read completed output |
| `ReadFile`, `WriteFile` | File operations |
| `ListDirectory` | List directory contents |

## Example Commands

```
@infected Execute: ls -la
@infected Run: npm install
@infected Execute: git status
@infected Run: docker ps
```

## Server Endpoints

| Endpoint | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `/mcp` | MCP HTTP Stream transport |
| `/sse` | MCP SSE transport |
| `/streaming/sse/{outputId}` | Real-time command output streaming |
| `/health` | Health check |

## Troubleshooting

### Server not starting
```bash
cd /root/sandbox/infected
npm start
```

### Check server health
```bash
curl http://localhost:3001/health
```

### Test client
```bash
npx tsx clients/test-client.ts "echo 'Hello'"
```

## Test Streaming

Run this command to see real-time streaming:

```bash
npx tsx clients/test-client.ts "for i in \$(seq 1 100); do echo \$i; sleep 0.5; done"
```

You should see numbers 1-100 printed one by one with 0.5 second intervals.

